Negative control for an open panel being switchable FROM INSIDE ITSELF
(T3.2 · `AC3.1` · `P1-R5`).

The sheet overlays and the page keeps its full width, which is `P1-R5` and stays
so — but the consequence is that the panel sits over the right of the page, and
the scenario bar's own Debug │ Code │ Scenario toggle is under it. Measured on a
1440 viewport: the panel takes the page from x≈1057, the bar's toggles sit at
x≈1150–1360, and `document.elementFromPoint` at a toggle's centre returns a row
inside the panel — the click is intercepted by the panel's own subtree. So with
any sheet open the only control that could change pane cannot be reached: the
panel has to be closed and reopened to switch, and the last of the scene rail's
stops is under the same panel.

The switcher inside the panel is the way out that costs the page no width. This
mutant removes it and puts the panel back to header-less, changing nothing else:
the host still renders every pane, still holds no open state of its own, still
reads `sheet=` off the url, and every existing claim about which pane is drawn,
which tabs the registry gives it and the page keeping its width stays green —
which is precisely why the loss is invisible without a claim of its own.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the T3.2 case asserting the OPEN panel offers the three sheets from within
itself — one entry per sheet inside the portalled panel, the open one marked,
and choosing another switching the pane while the panel stays open (an entry
that toggled would close the panel the pointer is inside). The bar's own toggle
is a different control with a different handle (`sheet-toggle` / `sheet`); this
one is `sheet-switcher` / `sheet-pane`, so a spec can never mistake one for the
other, and both draw the same `SHEET_LABELS` catalogue.

  Proven RED: owed by the prover's blind run (`vitest run --project component
  sheet-host`). Recorded here rather than pre-filled — the seat that authors the
  mutant is not the seat that grades it.

